{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And he was there with YHWH.</b> The expression עם ה' (with YHWH) indicates that he was sustained from the attribute of חסד and from the radiance of the supernal light, for it is written (Psalms 136): ״כי עמ ה׳ החסד״, and it is written (Daniel 2): ״ונהורא עמיה שרא״. And likewise the righteous in the world to come live and are sustained from the attribute of חסד and from the radiance of the supernal light, as it is written (Psalms 36:8–10): ״מה יקר חסדך אלהים ובני אדם בצל כנפיך יחסיון ירויון מדשן ביתך ונחל עדניך תשקם כי עמך מקור חיים באורך נראה אור״. And as for what it says, <b>bread he did not eat and water he did not drink</b>, it is known that bread and water are the sustenance of the body, and because of the body the soul persists and endures in the body; and since this is so, it is fitting a fortiori that the body should stand and be sustained by reason of the sustenance of the soul. And since Moshe was sustained from the radiance of the supernal light, which is the sustenance of the soul, there is no reason to marvel if he remained there all that time without food and drink. And the truth is that the sustenance of the body comes from cause after cause, and the intermediaries and causes are many, up to the supreme Cause, exalted be He, who is the source of life; if so, Moshe, who was joined to the supreme Cause itself, that is the true sustenance. And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory said on the verse (Exodus 24): ״ויחזו את האלהים ויאכלו וישתו״ — Rabbi Yohanan said: it was actual eating, for it is said (Proverbs 16): ״באור פני מלך חיים״." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״And he was there with YHWH forty days and forty nights״ — from what did Moshe know when it was day and when it was night? When the Holy One, blessed be He, would teach him the Written Torah, he knew it was day; and when He taught him the Oral Torah, he knew it was night, for day and night are equal before the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Daniel 2): ״ונהורא עמיה שרא״, and it is written (Psalms 139): ״גם חשך לא יחשיך״, etc. And they further expounded: when he saw the stars and constellations kneeling and prostrating themselves before the Holy One, blessed be He, he knew it was day; and when he saw the sphere of the sun kneeling and prostrating itself before the Holy One, blessed be He, he knew it was night, as it is said (Nehemiah 9): ״וצבא השמים לך משתחוים״." "<b>And he wrote on the tablets.</b> The subject returns to YHWH mentioned earlier, not to Moshe, perish the thought; and it is explicit in the verse: ״פסל לך שני לוחות אבנים כראשונים״, and ״וכתבתי״ — this indicates that the second tablets too were of divine writing. And it should have said ״וכתבתי על הלוחות הדברים״, and what it added — the word את — is inclusive, to include ״למען ייטב לך״ in the latter tablets, since in the former tablets there was no letter טי״ת in them; and I have already mentioned this in the portion וישמע יתרו. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ״ויהי שם עם ה׳ ויכתוב על הלוחות״ — for while Moshe was still with YHWH, YHWH wrote on the tablets." "<b>And behold.</b> These forty days were the third set of forty, for he had already spent there a second forty days praying concerning that sin, as it is written in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 9): ״ואתנפל לפני ה׳ את ארבעים היום ואת ארבעים הלילה אשר התנפלתי״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:14.878793633Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}